## 3. Module Layer (HTTP-exposed)

Modules follow **vertical slice architecture**. Every HTTP feature is a module.

### Required File Structure

```
modules/<name>/
├── <name>.router.ts       # Route definitions + middleware chain
├── <name>.controller.ts   # HTTP handlers only
├── <name>.service.ts      # Thin orchestration layer
├── <name>.repo.ts         # Database operations
├── <name>.schema.ts       # Drizzle table schema
├── <name>.dto.ts          # Zod schemas + TypeScript types
└── tests/
    ├── <name>.api.test.ts
    ├── <name>.db.test.ts
    └── <name>.unit.test.ts
```

### Module Service Pattern

Module services are thin — they **orchestrate, not compute**. Heavy logic is
delegated to internal services:

```typescript
// modules/evaluation/evaluation.service.ts
import { createQueue } from "@src/libs/queue/bullmq.ts";
import { evaluationRepo } from "./evaluation.repo.ts";
import { coreService } from "@src/intenals/core/core.service.ts";

export const evaluationService = {
  async create(data: CreateEvaluationBody): Promise<EvaluationDTO> {
    // 1. Create DB record with pending status
    const record = await evaluationRepo.create({
      ...data,
      status: EvaluationStatus.PENDING,
    });

    // 2. Queue background job — do NOT do heavy work here
    await createQueue().add(QUEUE_NAMES.WORKER_EVALUATE, {
      evaluationId: record.id,
      url: data.url,
      query: data.query,
    });

    return record;
  },
};
```

**Rule**: If a module service method does more than one of: DB write, queue add,
internal service call — it should likely be in `intenals/` instead.

### When to Create a Module (vs Internal Service)

| Module (`modules/`)             | Internal (`intenals/`)                       |
| ------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |
| Feature has HTTP endpoints      | Feature has no HTTP endpoints                |
| Exposes CRUD or webhook         | Pure business logic                          |
| Example: `evaluation`, `health` | Example: `core`, `web`, `embedding`, `chunk` |

