## 4. Internal Service Layer (Business Logic)

Internal services live in `src/intenals/` (note: typo "intenals" is
intentional). No router, no controller, no dto file.

### Factory Pattern (Mandatory)

Every internal service uses a **factory function** that returns a service
object. No classes, no `new`, no `this`. Dependencies are injected via the
factory:

```typescript
// intenals/web/web.service.ts
export const createWebService = (deps?: WebServiceDeps) => {
  const jina = deps?.jina ?? createJinaClient();
  const firecrawl = deps?.firecrawl ?? createFirecrawlClient();
  const fetcher = deps?.fetcher ?? globalThis.fetch;

  return {
    async getContent(url: string): Promise<WebContent> {
      // Multi-layer fallback: Jina → Firecrawl → Local
      try {
        return await jina.extract(url);
      } catch {
        try {
          return await firecrawl.scrape(url);
        } catch {
          return await localFetch(url, fetcher);
        }
      }
    },
  };
};

export type WebService = ReturnType<typeof createWebService>;
export type WebServiceDeps = {
  jina?: ReturnType<typeof createJinaClient>;
  firecrawl?: ReturnType<typeof createFirecrawlClient>;
  fetcher?: typeof fetch;
};
```

### Module-Level Singleton vs Per-Call Factory

- **Module-level**: `export const webService = createWebService();` — use when
  the service is stateless and has no per-call dependencies
- **Per-call factory**: `const service = createWebService(deps)` — use in tests
  or when the service needs custom configuration per call

### Internal Service Directory Structure

```
intenals/<name>/
├── <name>.service.ts      # Factory + business logic
├── <name>.repo.ts         # DB operations (only if needed)
├── <name>.engine.ts       # External API client (only if needed)
└── tests/
    ├── <name>.unit.test.ts
    └── <name>.repo.test.ts  # Only if repo exists
```

